          }
        },
        "reason": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "optional: in a sentence, why this answer (kept with the task's decision when a supervision message handed you the card)"
        }
      }
    },
    "core_rank": 28
  },
  {
    "name": "ask_escalate",
    "description": "Hand the cards a task's agents wait on to the user, when a supervision message gave them to you first and the user must decide: a tool permission or a plan (no conversation approves those), or a question that is irreversible, seen by other people, changes the goal or goes against a decision already made on the task. Every card of the task the user has not been told of goes to them at once, as one reminder (in the user's push grading it pushes only when why_owner or p0 says it is theirs alone), with your reason and recommendation on it; their answers go straight to the agents and are kept as the task's decisions. Only the task's supervisor and the main conversation can.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "todo_id",
        "reason"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "todo_id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the task's todo id (todo_…), from the supervision message"
        },
        "reason": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "in a sentence, in the user's language: what they are to decide and why it is theirs"
        },
        "recommend": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the option you recommend, in a line (an option's label when there is one)"
        },
        "why_owner": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "irreversible",
            "visible_to_others",
            "priority",
            "acceptance",
            "only_owner_knows"
          ],
          "description": "why only the user can decide it. In the user's push grading only irreversible, visible_to_others and only_owner_knows send them a notification; the rest wait in their Needs you list and briefing"
        },
        "p0": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "true: the user must do or decide this themselves now, so it pushes even in push grading whatever why_owner says"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_suggest",
    "description": "Submit the event’s recap and one suggestion, replacing any earlier suggestion. It waits in the user's inbox; nothing goes out until they confirm, and then the program does exactly what it says (you never send it yourself). `kind`: `reply` — one message to send, `text` in the user's voice, ready to go out as it is (no blanks to fill in like 【…】: leave out what you could not find and say so in `note`; for a Feishu sheet / Base item `changes` proposes moving rows' status: ref, field and one of the values the item lists); `agent` — an Agent to start, `text` its instruction (`machine` and `cwd` optional); `todo` — a to-do for the user to add: `text` its title (clear, starting with the action, no chat or people names); the user can edit it and adds it with one tap (a row that came to a to-do counts as handled); `conclusion` — you dealt with it, `text` the conclusion, `outcome` `fyi` (worth knowing) or `done` (nothing to do). `handback: true` for one the user has to judge: their confirmation comes back here as an instruction instead of being carried out. `note`: one or two sentences on what you found and where. `recap`: the event's background, progress and where it is stuck (up to 600 characters), every time. `title`: what the matter is, without chat or person names; `summary`: one sentence on where things stand and who is waited on for what. Write both every time; items whose title comes from the source (mail, documents, PRs / issues, and the like) ignore `title`, and the tool says so. `related`: ids of inbox items in other chats or sources this one is about. `recap` without `kind` creates no suggestion, and may include `title`, `summary`, and `follow`. `follow` records waiting on the other side: `who` (empty means waiting for a reply), `next`, and `check_at` (RFC 3339 with a time zone) or `check_in` (such as `30m`, `4h`, or `2d`). `check_at` takes precedence; with neither, look again in `24h`; the resulting time is clamped to 30 minutes–14 days from now. `follow` is valid only without `kind`. `reported`: `{text, by}` records one externally visible action you or an Agent actually completed but the program did not observe, without verifying it; never use it for plans or actions not taken. `by` is `agent` or `assistant` (default `assistant`), and `text` is one sentence, kept on one line and clipped to 120 characters. It may be given alone or accompany `kind`, `recap`, `title`, `summary`, or `follow`; a filed item refuses it, and text identical to the item's latest `reported` entry is not recorded twice. Call it at the end of each turn. In a conversation the user handed an inbox item to (its context block names the item's id), pass that id as `item` and call it when you have something to write back to the item, and again whenever it changes (it also shows in that conversation as a card); it refuses an item not handed to this conversation or one the user already filed: answer in the chat then.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "processing_turn": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "item": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "reply",
            "agent",
            "conclusion",
            "todo"
          ]
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "note": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "handback": {
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "outcome": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "fyi",
            "done"
          ]
        },
        "recap": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "reported": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": [
            "text"
          ],
          "properties": {
            "text": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "by": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "agent",
                "assistant"
              ],
              "default": "assistant"
            }
          }
        },
        "title": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "summary": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "follow": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "who": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "next": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "check_at": {
              "type": "string",
              "format": "date-time"
            },
            "check_in": {
              "type": "string"
            }
          }
        },
        "machine": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "cwd": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "related": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "changes": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "required": [
              "ref",
              "field",
              "value"
            ],
            "properties": {
              "ref": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "field": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "value": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "inbox_folders",
    "description": "List the folders the user's inbox items are filed under: each `/`-separated path (`work/alerts/engine`, every segment a level) with its item count and open count, and whether automatic filing by the triage model is on. Give `folder` to also list the items filed in that folder and below, or `unfiled: true` for the items in no folder; each line has the item's id (what inbox_move takes), source, title, sender and whether it is done. Call it before inbox_move, to reuse a folder in use rather than open a near-duplicate.",
