  {
    "name": "inbox_post",
    "description": "Put this conversation into the user's Inbox as a row that opens it, so a result is not missed behind a quiet unread dot. Call it only when the user explicitly asked for it (for example \"tell me if there is anything worth a look\", \"send me the briefing every time\") or when the scheduled task you are running is set to put findings in the Inbox. Never decide on your own to interrupt the user: an ordinary reply, a run with nothing new, or something merely interesting does not go there. `title` is a short name for the row (the task's name or what was found), `summary` one or two sentences saying what to look at, `tier` is `action` when the user has to do something and `fyi` (the default) when it is worth knowing, and `reason` names the request or task setting that makes this post (the user can read it on the row). Calling it again for this conversation soon after updates the same row instead of adding another. It does not send a notification: use notify_user for that when the user asked for a push.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "title",
        "summary",
        "reason"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "title": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "summary": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "tier": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "action",
            "fyi"
          ]
        },
        "reason": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "persona_settings",
    "description": "This is how the user changes Persona's settings: ask them in conversation, there is no settings page for these. `list` (the default) shows every key with a one-line description, how to set it and its current value (secrets only as set or unset); `get` reads one. `set`, `add` and `remove` show before → after as a confirmation card in Persona and change nothing until they approve; secrets are entered there, never in chat. Change only what was asked. Areas: assistant behavior (tasks, supervision and close-out, idle compaction and release, auto-naming, cache keep-alive, suggestions, folds, the computer and launcher the main chat runs on), session triage (`agents.triage.*`), About Me, saved prompts, start page and sidebar, inbox sorting and task links, mail accounts, extensions (`extension.\u003cid\u003e`, which include channels and connectors) and their download mirrors, hub update channel and auto-update, device notifications, each agent CLI's own settings per computer (`agent_settings.\u003ccli\u003e`) and muted Claude config directories. Model profiles are not here: use `model_profiles_list`, `model_profile_get`, `model_profile_switch` and `model_profile_rollback`.\n\nModel groups `model_config.kb`, `model_config.inbox.triage`, `model_config.agent.delegate` and `model_config.translate`: `get` returns their effective binding, revision and compatible `purpose.provider_ids`. `set` and `test` take one listed provider ID as value. A changed provider clears the old model, effort and permission mode. `test` only checks the connection, without approval or saving; an unverified or offline provider may still be saved.\n\n`assistant.standing_authorizations`: only the main conversation may replace the list at its current revision with the user’s approval in Persona. Each grant names `machine_id`, absolute `directory`, `actions` (merge, push, deploy-test, deploy-production, send, delete, spend), `conditions`, exact user `quote`, `source` and `reversible`. Keep unchanged grants by ID; replace one by removing it and adding it without an ID. To revoke a grant, replace the list without that grant. Never invent a quote or expand scope. Only independently verifiable conditions may permit automatic answers. Tool permissions are never automatically approved.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "list",
            "get",
            "set",
            "test",
            "add",
            "remove"
          ],
          "description": "default list"
        },
        "key": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the setting, from list (assistant.*, agents.triage.*, navigation.*, hub.*, inbox.rules, mail.accounts, extension.\u003cid\u003e…)"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": [
            "string",
            "number",
            "integer",
            "boolean",
            "object",
            "array",
            "null"
          ],
          "description": "set: the new value; test: the provider ID to check; add: the item (each setting's list entry says what)"
        },
        "id": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "remove: which item (from get)"
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "core_rank": 4
  },
  {
    "name": "secret_run",
    "description": "Run commands on one of the user's computers that need a password only the user knows, typically its sudo password. Never ask for a password in the conversation or in ask_user: call this instead. The user gets a card in the Persona app showing the computer, your purpose and every command verbatim, types the password there and taps Run; that computer's node then runs exactly those commands in order, each as the user with /bin/sh -c in the home directory and PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin (give other tools by their absolute path). Only a command that runs sudo with -S gets the password, as a line on its stdin; every other command gets an empty stdin. Write sudo as `sudo -S -k -p '' …`, one sudo per command and never after a pipe (sudo would read the pipe; run a pipeline as root with `sudo -S -k -p '' sh -c '…'`); a sudo without -S is refused. Where a sudoers rule says NOPASSWD, sudo does not read stdin and the command it runs gets the password instead, so do not give such a command anything that reads stdin. Commands are printable ASCII only, so the card shows exactly what runs. The first command that fails stops the run. Never write a command that prints, stores or sends the password; you never see it. The result is JSON with status (ran, failed, declined, expired or refused), the computer, a message and, for ran and failed, each command's exit_code, timed_out and output (stdout and stderr, clipped to its end); when a command's output held the password, none of it comes back (output_withheld: true). A command stopped at the timeout may still run as root: check before asking again. declined, expired and refused mean nothing ran: tell the user; ask again only if they want. Not on Windows.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "machine",
        "purpose",
        "commands"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "machine": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "the computer to run on: its id or name, as agent_start takes it"
        },
        "purpose": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "one sentence the card shows: why the password is needed"
        },
        "commands": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "minItems": 1,
          "maxItems": 5,
          "description": "1 to 5 one-line shell commands of printable ASCII, at most 1000 characters each, run in order"
        },
        "delivery": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "stdin"
          ],
          "description": "how the password reaches the commands: stdin, the default and only way"
        },
        "timeout_seconds": {
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 1800,
          "description": "for the whole run, default 300"
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
  {
    "name": "skill_import",
    "description": "Import a user-requested skill ZIP through Persona's existing Settings importer. First preview the base64-encoded archive (up to 4 MiB compressed); returns validated metadata, a short-lived reference and environment. Then install with the same archive, reference and environment. Installation and undo wait for the user's own permission card showing the validated skill and target computer; never approve for the user. The target is the current assistant's configured personal computer, never a model-chosen path or machine. Skills are shared by assistants using that computer account and load in new conversations; an existing conversation is not guaranteed to refresh. Same-name skills are never overwritten. ZIP inspection does not run scripts or treat skill instructions as authorization. Use status with the original reference and environment after a lost/uncertain response, before any retry: it records previewed, installed or undone and recovers the undo receipt. Status is an operation receipt, not a live inventory; receipts expire after ten minutes or a computer restart. If unavailable, inspect Settings \u003e Skills instead of assuming failure. Cancel discards an unused preview. Undo takes an installed receipt, refuses edited/replaced files and retains recovery data. No URL download, repository clone, arbitrary path write or arbitrary skill removal is provided. An older computer may require a Persona update.",
    "input_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": [
        "op"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "op": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "preview",
            "install",
            "status",
            "cancel",
            "undo"
          ]
        },
        "archive": {
          "type": "string",
          "maxLength": 5592408,
          "description": "Base64 ZIP bytes, required only for preview and install. Supply identical bytes for both; do not put secrets in the archive."
        },
        "reference": {
          "type": "string",
          "maxLength": 256,
          "description": "Opaque receipt from preview/install, required except for preview. Never invent one."
        },
        "environment": {
          "type": "string",
          "maxLength": 128,
          "description": "Environment returned by preview, required except for preview. A changed assistant computer requires a fresh preview."
        }
      }
    },
    "defer_loading": true
  },
